An NFS version 3 server for FreeMiNT
====================================

Three programs that let an Atari export directories to other machines:

  portmap	the port mapper, program 100000 version 2, on port 111.
		Clients ask it where the other two are, so it has to be
		started first.
  mountd	the MOUNT protocol, program 100005 version 3. Hands out the
		first file handle for an export.
  nfsd		NFS version 3 itself, program 100003, on port 2049.

This is the other direction from sys/xfs/nfs3, which is the client: that one
mounts somebody else's export onto the Atari, this one offers the Atari's own
directories to somebody else. The two are independent and can run at once.


STARTING IT
===========

Write /etc/exports first; see exports.example for the format and for what
the options mean. Then, in this order:

    portmap
    mountd
    nfsd

Each one registers itself with the port mapper and refuses to start if it
cannot, so a failure to start says what is wrong instead of leaving a
half working server. Common options:

    -d		log every request to stderr. Verbose, but it is the
		quickest way to see which procedure a client is unhappy
		with.
    -e file	read a different exports file
    -p port	listen on a particular port. For nfsd the default is 2049,
		for mountd it is whatever the system gives out.
    -n		do not register with the port mapper. Only useful for
		testing, with a client that is told the ports directly.

Check it from the other machine with

    rpcinfo -p <atari>
    showmount -e <atari>

and mount it the usual way:

    mount -t nfs -o vers=3 <atari>:/c/shared /mnt


WHAT IS IMPLEMENTED
===================

21 of the 22 procedures of NFS version 3: NULL, GETATTR, SETATTR, LOOKUP,
ACCESS, READLINK, READ, WRITE, CREATE, MKDIR, SYMLINK, REMOVE, RMDIR,
RENAME, LINK, READDIR, READDIRPLUS, FSSTAT, FSINFO, PATHCONF and COMMIT.
The one left out is MKNOD, which answers NFS3ERR_NOTSUPP: there is no
portable way to make a device node and nothing on an Atari wants one over
NFS.

Both UDP and TCP, on the same port number, with RPC record marking and
reassembly for TCP. One process, one select() loop, no forking: a request is
answered before the next is looked at.

AUTH_UNIX and AUTH_NONE. There is no Kerberos, which is also true of the
client driver and of NFSv3 as most people run it.


FILE HANDLES, AND WHY THEY LOOK LIKE THAT
=========================================

A handle is the thing a client keeps and hands back for every operation on a
file, and it has to keep working across a server restart or clients get
ESTALE and have to remount.

Linux builds its handles from a device number, an inode number and a
generation count, and relies on the kernel being able to find a file by
inode. Userspace cannot do that: there is no portable way from an inode
number back to a path. The usual answer is a cache of issued handles, which
then has to be persisted somewhere to survive a restart.

This server puts the path itself in the handle, relative to its export.
Nothing is cached, nothing is written, nothing goes stale when the server is
restarted, and there is no table to size on a machine with 4 MB. The price is
a limit on depth: NFSD_FHPATHLEN in config.h, 56 bytes, which is what is left
of the 64 byte maximum after the magic, the export number and the length.
A file deeper than that cannot be reached, and says so with
NFS3ERR_NAMETOOLONG rather than failing in some less obvious way.

Because the path travels through the client, it comes back under the
client's control, so a decoded handle is treated as hostile input: a
relative path, no empty components, no "." or "..", nothing with a separator
inside a component. That confines a handle to the export it names, and
inside that export a client could reach the same file with LOOKUP anyway.
There is no cryptographic check and none would help -- it would need a
secret that itself had to survive a restart.


WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT PROTECT
===================================

The server does its work as whatever user it runs as, which on an Atari is
the only user there is. Before each operation it checks the file's mode bits
against the uid the request claims, so a read only file behaves like one and
an export marked ro really refuses writes.

That check is not a security boundary. The uid in an NFSv3 request is the
client's own assertion about itself, with nothing to back it up, so anyone
who can reach the port and set a uid field can present any identity. The
boundary is the address list in /etc/exports. Treat an export the way you
would treat a directory shared with every machine on that list.

The port mapper leaves out PMAP_CALLIT, which asks it to make an RPC call on
the caller's behalf and send back the answer: a one packet request with a
forged source address produces a large reply aimed at somebody else. NFS has
no use for it. PMAP_SET and PMAP_UNSET are accepted only from the loopback
address, so a registration cannot be redirected from the network.


LIMITS
======

  - Files are limited to 2 GB, because MiNT's file position is a signed
    32 bit value. The size in an attribute reply is a 64 bit field and is
    reported truthfully, but a READ or WRITE past 2 GB is refused with
    NFS3ERR_INVAL or NFS3ERR_FBIG. FSINFO reports maxfilesize accordingly.
  - The largest READ or WRITE is NFSD_MAXDATA, 8192 bytes by default, which
    is announced through FSINFO so clients use it. Raising it costs memory
    per connection.
  - At most NFSD_MAXEXPORTS exports and RPC_MAXCONN TCP connections at once;
    a further connection is refused rather than displacing an established
    one. Both are in config.h.
  - CREATE in EXCLUSIVE mode behaves like GUARDED. Doing it properly means
    storing the client's verifier with the file so that a retry after a lost
    reply can be told apart from a second attempt, and there is nowhere to
    put it. The difference shows only in that case, where this returns
    NFS3ERR_EXIST instead of success.
  - MKNOD is not implemented.
  - No file locking: there is no NLM, so two clients writing the same file
    are not protected from each other.
  - The READDIR cookie counts entries rather than using a directory offset,
    so a listing that is continued re-reads the directory from the start.
    The cookie verifier is sent as zeroes, which means the server does not
    claim to detect a directory that changed between calls.
  - A client's uid is not assumed by the server process, so file ownership
    of newly created files is the server's, adjusted by the export's squash
    options.


TESTING
=======

"make check" builds and runs the two unit tests with the host compiler:
the XDR core and the file handle encoding, including every path traversal
the handle validation has to refuse.

Three more tests drive the daemons over a socket, which means they can run
on the machine the code is built on, against the host build:

    cc -o nfsd   nfsd.c   rpc.c xdr.c fh.c exports.c -DHAVE_FSYNC -DHAVE_STATVFS
    cc -o mountd mountd.c rpc.c xdr.c fh.c exports.c
    cc -o portmap portmap.c rpc.c xdr.c

    ./t_portmap.py                       # starts its own portmap

    ./mountd -n -p 20048 -e /tmp/exports &
    ./nfsd   -n -p 20490 -e /tmp/exports &
    ./t_nfsd_libnfs.py   /tmp/nfsroot 20490 20048
    ./t_nfsd_security.py /tmp/nfsroot 20490 20048

t_nfsd_libnfs.py drives the server with libnfs, which is a complete NFSv3
client written by somebody else: a test that passes there says the server
follows the protocol rather than that it agrees with its own client.

t_nfsd_security.py forges file handles by hand -- naming "..", absolute
paths, lying about lengths -- because no real client library will build an
invalid handle, and that is exactly what needs testing.

t_portmap.py encodes the port mapper protocol itself, in Python, rather than
calling this package's own code.


STATUS
======

Verified on the host: 33 XDR checks, 53 file handle checks, 29 port mapper
checks, 45 protocol checks against libnfs covering read and write, and 26
handle forgery attempts, all passing.

On hardware: the three daemons run on a Falcon, and from a Linux host
"rpcinfo -p" lists all six registrations while "showmount -e" returns the
export list. That covers the part that could not be tested here -- the
FreeMiNT socket layer, select() over a mix of UDP and TCP sockets, and the
RPC record assembly on a TCP connection.

What has had little exercise is everything beyond that first contact:
sustained reading and writing, several clients at once, and what happens
when a client disappears mid-request. Expect to find things there.
